-
-  <section class="strength-section section-mist"><div class="shell strength-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">The business behind the work</p><h2>Built around what I am actually good at.</h2></div><div><p>I work best when I can take something substantial, understand what makes it work and refine it independently. Starting <em>every</em> project from a blank page added uncertainty without automatically adding value &mdash; so the collection became the default, and the blank page became a deliberate choice rather than a habit.</p><p>That is why there are two routes rather than one. Most practices are better served by a strong direction, tailored properly, at a price published in advance. Some need the thinking done first, and an original design that comes out of it. Both get the same standard of craft.</p><blockquote>Less uncertainty for the client. More focused craft from me. No reduction in quality.</blockquote></div></div></section>
-
-  <section class="beliefs-section section-paper"><div class="shell"><div class="section-heading-row"><div><p class="eyebrow">What guides the Studio</p><h2>Professional does not have to mean generic.</h2></div><p>A therapy website has practical work to do, but it also creates a room in the visitor&apos;s mind. Good design makes the practice easier to recognise without making claims the therapist cannot honestly hold.</p></div>
-    <div class="belief-grid"><article><span>01</span><h3>Let people feel it</h3><p>A person often knows whether a website feels safe, clear or credible before they can explain why. The visual atmosphere matters.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Make the next step plain</h3><p>Fees, location, suitability and the enquiry route should not be hidden behind branding language.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Do not overstate</h3><p>The website should make the real quality of a practice visible, not manufacture a grander version of it.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Keep the process contained</h3><p>Written questions, clear approval points and consolidated revisions make creative work calmer for everyone.</p></article></div>
-  </div></section>
-
-  <section class="two-lenses section-mist"><div class="shell two-lenses-grid"><div class="lens-card"><p class="eyebrow">Website craft</p><h2>I notice hierarchy, rhythm and the point where a visitor gets lost.</h2><p>I care about typography, responsive behaviour, accessibility, performance and search fundamentals—but I use them in service of a clear human experience.</p></div><div class="lens-card lens-card-dark"><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Counselling understanding</p><h2>I know that trust is not produced by soft colours and the word “safe.”</h2><p>My training helps me question generic therapeutic language, notice the emotional demand a page places on someone, and treat the enquiry journey with care.</p></div></div></section>
